<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#intercession-of-the-theotokos">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Intercession of the Theotokos</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pokrov</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Intercession of the Theotokos (Pokrov) is an Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> feast, celebrated on 1 October (14 October in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregorian-calendar">Gregorian calendar</a>), commemorating a vision of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a> spreading her veil over the faithful in the Blachernae church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. Though based on a Byzantine story, the feast was established in Rus', traditionally by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrei-bogolyubsky">Andrei Bogolyubsky</a> in the twelfth century, and became one of the most important in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/intercession-of-the-theotokos.jpg" alt="Intercession of the Theotokos" width="320" height="378" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Icon_02041_Pokrov._Ikona._XIX_v._Rossiya.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to Eastern Orthodox Sacred Tradition, the apparition took place in the tenth century at the Blachernae church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, where relics of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a>, her robe, her veil and part of her belt, were kept. At four in the morning on Sunday, 1 October, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-the-apostle">Saint Andrew</a> the Blessed, a Fool-for-Christ and a Slav by birth, saw the dome of the church open and the Virgin enter, surrounded by angels and saints. She prayed with tears for all faithful Christians, then spread her veil over everyone in the church as a protection. Andrew asked his disciple Epiphanius whether he saw it, and Epiphanius said that he did.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nestor">Nestor</a> the Chronicler tells another story: the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> called on the Mother of God to protect them from a large Rus' army, then still pagan, and the feast celebrates the destruction of that fleet in the ninth century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Slavonic name Pokrov and the Greek Sképē mean a cloak or shroud as well as protection or intercession. The feast falls on 14 October or 1 October and is served as an all-night vigil, but without an afterfeast. Veneration of the Pokrov in Rus' dates to the twelfth century. In the Slavic Orthodox Churches it ranks as the most important solemnity after the Twelve Great Feasts and Pascha, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oriental-orthodox-churches">Oriental Orthodox Churches</a> do not keep it.</p><p>Its icon, not found in Byzantine art, shows the Virgin in a luminous aureole holding a veil in her outstretched arms, with saints and angels on either side and Andrew and Epiphanius below.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> the Intercession became the third most important holiday after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a>, linked to the Ukrainian Cossacks, whose Zaporozhian Host took the Virgin as patroness. A presidential decree of 1999 made the day Ukrainian Cossack Day, and on 14 October 2014 another decree made it Defenders of Ukraine Day.</p><p>Many churches carry the name, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-basils-cathedral">Saint Basil's Cathedral</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, officially the Church of the Intercession on the Moat, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-intercession-on-the-nerl">Church of the Intercession on the Nerl</a> near Vladimir. Both are on the World Heritage List.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Intercession of the Theotokos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Orthodox Christianity spread outwards from the towns, and Andrei Bogolyubsky promoted the cult of the Virgin, introducing the <b>feast of the Intercession</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vladimir-suzdal">Vladimir-Suzdal · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
